#
# What this file is: the layout every one of the five ATRIUM tool repos' repo-root
# `.env.example` follows. atrium-project#60. Copy this file to `<repo>/.env.example`,
# delete this meta block, and fill in every <placeholder>.
#
# Who must follow it: all five service repos (atrium-alto-postprocess,
# atrium-llm-enrich, atrium-nlp-enrich, atrium-page-classification, atrium-translator).
# Checked mechanically by tests/test_env_contract.py, vendored byte-identically into
# each repo from docs/templates/shared/test_env_contract.py and enforced by
# para-drift.reusable.yml — a repo's .env.example that stops matching this layout's
# invariants fails CI, not a human review.
#
# THE THREE NORMATIVE RULES. These are the teeth; everything else below is layout.
#
#   Rule 1 — STOCK DEFAULT. Every UNCOMMENTED value is the code default, verbatim.
#     Why: docker-tool.reusable.yml's CI lane runs `cp .env.example .env` in every
#     repo, so a "hardened example" value silently becomes the CI configuration — and
#     in any repo whose compose declares `env_file: .env`, the container's real
#     configuration. A hardened example belongs in a comment, in prose, never as the
#     live value. (The one exception a repo may need: a variable whose compose file
#     supplies a narrower default of its own for local development safety — ship the
#     CODE default here and name the compose default in the same comment; do not
#     silently ship the compose value as if it were universal.)
#
#   Rule 2 — BLANK SAFETY. `VAR=` (blank) ships ONLY when that variable's own resolver
#     treats blank as unset. Verified per variable, not assumed:
#       - safe (blank -> default): URL overrides using `or default` / `.strip()`
#         truthiness (UDPIPE_URL, NAMETAG_URL, TRANSLATION_URL, LINDAT_*, LLM_*, CT2_*
#         helpers), RELOAD, API_KEEP_WORKSPACES
#       - safe by accident: MAX_UPLOAD_MB (float("") raises, is caught, falls through)
#       - SILENTLY WRONG: ALLOWED_ORIGINS — `os.getenv(...).split(",")` filtered by
#         `if o.strip()` yields an EMPTY origin list, blocking every browser request.
#         Omitting the line gives you `*`. Opposite outcomes. Ship it COMMENTED.
#       - CRASHES AT STARTUP: PORT, GRACEFUL_SHUTDOWN_S (`int("")`), LOG_LEVEL
#         (`basicConfig(level="")`), and any variable read with a bare `int(os.getenv(...))`
#         and no try/except.
#     When in doubt, ship the variable COMMENTED rather than blank.
#
#   Rule 3 — COMPUTED DEFAULT. A variable whose default is computed at runtime (a path
#     derived from the repo root, a value read out of another config file) ships
#     COMMENTED, with the default described in PROSE. A literal here would freeze a
#     path that is only correct for one working directory.
#
# TAG LEGEND — one of these seven, in every per-entry comment:
#   [required]    the service raises at startup without it
#   [secret]      never a literal in a manifest or a committed file; use a k8s Secret
#   [operator]    an ordinary deployment knob
#   [algorithmic] changes what the service COMPUTES, not how it is deployed — ships
#                 commented, in §6, never in the k8s partner-facing table
#   [build]       read by compose/Dockerfile, or baked at build time (ATRIUM_*, HF_HOME)
#   [deprecated]  ships commented; today only MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES
#   [unreachable] code exists, no registry path reaches it today (e.g. translator's CT2_*)
#
# COMMENT IDIOM: `# [tag] <one sentence>. Default: <code default>.` plus at most one
# more line. Longer explanations belong in the SECTION preamble, not stacked under one
# variable — see nlp-enrich's §3 preamble for the model.
#
# SECTION RULE: all seven sections below are present in EVERY repo's file, even when a
# section's body is a single "None — <why>." line. A missing knob then reads as
# DELIBERATE, not forgotten — this is the single biggest reason drift went unnoticed
# for months. page-classification's shortest-in-the-fleet file is the reference: "the
# SHORTEST of the five, and that is the correct output rather than a gap" is exactly
# the sentence every empty section should be able to say about itself.
#
# VERBATIM BLOCKS: §1, §2 and §4 below are byte-identical across all five repos except
# the MAX_UPLOAD_MB number and the entrypoint module name in comments. This is what
# keeps the guard cheap — five files that agree everywhere except where the code
# genuinely differs.
#
# WHAT MUST NOT GO IN THIS FILE:
#   - per-run / batch-pipeline knobs (config.txt, config_api.txt, llm_config.txt,
#     api_util/summarize_nt_udp.py's ~15 names) — declare them in tests/
#     env_contract_data.py's NOT_PUBLISHED with a reason instead
#   - the annotation-stack credentials in nlp-enrich/annotation/env.example — a
#     different stack, a different audience, its own template
#   - any REAL secret value, ever
#
# HOW THE DELIVERY PARAGRAPH BELOW WORKS: state, once, whether `docker compose up`
# actually injects this file into the container (env_file:) or merely uses it to
# expand ${VAR} inside the compose file itself (a DIFFERENT mechanism — substitution
# fills in the compose file, injection fills in the container). Kubernetes never reads
# this file at all; point at docs/k8s_deployment.md in ufal/atrium-project.
#
### END TEMPLATE META #############################################################

# atrium-<tool> — the published environment contract (atrium-project#60).
#
# Copy to `.env` and edit. Do not commit `.env` (gitignored at .gitignore:<line>).
# This file is the COMPLETE ledger: every variable the api image reads, at the
# default that is in the CODE. The operator-facing subset across all five services is
# the table in the hub's docs/k8s_deployment.md; this file's layout is fixed by
# docs/templates/env.example.template in ufal/atrium-project.
#
# HOW THIS FILE REACHES THE PROCESS
#   docker compose  → <delivered / NOT delivered — state which, and why>
#   Kubernetes      → NOT delivered. Use the manifest's own env: block. See
#                     ufal/atrium-project docs/k8s_deployment.md.
#   python -m <entrypoint>
#                   → NOT delivered. Nothing here calls load_dotenv(); export the
#                     values yourself:  set -a; . ./.env; set +a
#
# BLANK IS NOT UNIVERSALLY "UNSET". See Rule 2 above. ALLOWED_ORIGINS is the trap: a
# blank value parses to an EMPTY origin list and blocks every browser request, where
# omitting the line gives you `*`. PORT, GRACEFUL_SHUTDOWN_S and LOG_LEVEL are worse —
# each raises at startup. An entry that may safely ship blank says so on its own line;
# nothing else here ships blank.

# ── §0 Required before first start ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# None — this service starts with no variables set.
# <or the [required]/[secret] entries a backend needs before it will boot>

# ── §1 HTTP surface ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PORT=8000
# [operator] Bind address. Default "0.0.0.0". ⚠️ 127.0.0.1 yields a container that
# reports HEALTHY (service/healthcheck.py always probes loopback) while being
# unreachable from outside it — see docs/k8s_deployment.md's warning.
# HOST=0.0.0.0
# [operator] CSV of CORS origins. Code default "*" (every origin). An EMPTY value is
# NOT the same as omitting the line — see Rule 2 above. Ships commented so an operator
# who leaves it alone gets the real code default, not a value frozen here.
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=*
# [operator] Canonical upload limit in MB, resolved by
# service/atrium_service.py:resolve_max_upload_mb. THERE IS NO SHARED DEFAULT across
# the five services — see docs/k8s_deployment.md's per-repo table before copying a
# number from another repo's file.
MAX_UPLOAD_MB=<code default for this repo>
# [deprecated] Byte-valued fallback; loses to MAX_UPLOAD_MB whenever both are set. Do
# not set this in a new deployment.
# MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=
# [operator] The path service/healthcheck.py probes inside the container. Kubernetes
# never reads this (see docs/k8s_deployment.md's opening section) — change it only
# alongside the route it targets.
# HEALTHCHECK_PATH=/health

# ── §2 Lifecycle ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [operator] uvicorn's bound on waiting for in-flight requests. Raise it together
# with the k8s manifest's terminationGracePeriodSeconds.
# GRACEFUL_SHUTDOWN_S=20
# [operator] Root logger level for the entrypoint (DEBUG/INFO/WARNING/ERROR). All five
# services honour it identically (atrium-project#61); tests/test_logging_contract.py
# is vendored in each repo and fails if one drifts.
LOG_LEVEL=INFO
# [operator] uvicorn filesystem auto-reload. A development convenience — never set
# this in a deployment; there is no correct value for it in a Deployment spec.
# RELOAD=false

# ── §3 Backing services (atrium-project#63) ─────────────────────────────────────
# None — this service calls no attachable backing service.
# <or the per-service URL overrides, each documented [operator], each noting its
#  LINDAT-public-host fallback and that an empty value counts as unset>

# ── §4 Image / provenance ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [build] Read only by docker-compose.yml to pick the image tag; no Python here reads
# it.
ATRIUM_VERSION=dev
# [build] Baked at build time; read by atrium_paradata.py for provenance. Override
# only if you publish under another name.
ATRIUM_RUNNER_REF=dev
# [build] # ATRIUM_RUNNER_IMAGE=ghcr.io/ufal/atrium-<tool>:dev
# [build] # ATRIUM_RUNNER_REPO=https://github.com/ufal/atrium-<tool>
# [build] Read once at import via atrium_paradata.py's module-level constants — it
# identifies a batch RUN, not an HTTP request.
# ATRIUM_REQUEST_ID=
# [build] Read by huggingface_hub itself; paired with the hf-cache volume mount in the
# k8s manifest. Without the matching volume, every start re-downloads the model cache.
# HF_HOME=/cache/huggingface

# ── §5 Service knobs — operator ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# None — this service reads nothing beyond §1–§4.
# <or this repo's operator-facing variables, each [operator]/[required]/[secret]>

# ── §6 Completeness ledger — algorithmic, deprecated, unreachable ────────────────
# [algorithmic] / [unreachable] entries: read by shipped code, but changing what the
# service COMPUTES rather than how it is deployed, or not reachable via any registry
# path today. Commented, always — a deployment does not touch these.
